[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Scope in JavaScript (Global, Function, Block)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Scope in JavaScript determines the accessibility of variables. It essentially defines where in your code you can access and use a particular variable. Understanding scope is crucial for writing maintainable and bug-free JavaScript code. There are three main types of scope: Global, Function, and Block."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Global Scope"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Variables declared outside of any function or block have global scope. This means they can be accessed and modified from anywhere in your JavaScript code, including inside functions and blocks. However, excessive use of global variables can lead to naming conflicts and make your code harder to debug and maintain."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"// Global variable
var globalVariable = "I am global";

function myFunction() {
  console.log(globalVariable); // Can access globalVariable here
}

myFunction(); // Output: I am global

console.log(globalVariable); // Can access globalVariable here
"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Function Scope"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Variables declared inside a function have function scope. They are only accessible within that function. This helps to encapsulate data and prevent naming conflicts with variables outside the function. Variables declared with 'var' keyword inside a function are function scoped."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"function myFunction() {
  var functionVariable = "I am function scoped";
  console.log(functionVariable); // Can access functionVariable here
}

myFunction(); // Output: I am function scoped

//console.log(functionVariable); // Error: functionVariable is not defined (outside the function)
"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Block Scope"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Block scope was introduced with ES6 (ECMAScript 2015) using the 'let' and 'const' keywords. Variables declared with 'let' or 'const' inside a block (e.g., inside an 'if' statement, 'for' loop, or any code enclosed in curly braces '{}') are only accessible within that block. This provides better control over variable visibility and helps prevent accidental modification of variables from outside the block."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"if (true) {
  let blockVariable = "I am block scoped";
  const blockConstant = "I am also block scoped";
  console.log(blockVariable); // Can access blockVariable here
  console.log(blockConstant); // Can access blockConstant here
}

//console.log(blockVariable); // Error: blockVariable is not defined (outside the block)
//console.log(blockConstant); // Error: blockConstant is not defined (outside the block)
"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Note: Variables declared with 'var' are NOT block-scoped. They are either globally scoped (if declared outside a function) or function-scoped (if declared inside a function), regardless of whether they are declared inside a block."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"if (true) {
  var varVariable = "I am NOT block scoped";
}

console.log(varVariable); // Output: I am NOT block scoped (because 'var' is not block-scoped)
"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Scope Chain"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"When a variable is accessed, JavaScript searches for it in the current scope. If it's not found, it moves up the scope chain to the next outer scope, and so on, until it reaches the global scope. If the variable is still not found, an error is thrown (ReferenceError: variable is not defined)."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"var globalVar = "Global";

function outerFunction() {
  var outerVar = "Outer";

  function innerFunction() {
    var innerVar = "Inner";
    console.log(innerVar);   // Accesses innerVar (local scope)
    console.log(outerVar);   // Accesses outerVar (outer function scope)
    console.log(globalVar);  // Accesses globalVar (global scope)
  }

  innerFunction();
  //console.log(innerVar); // Error: innerVar is not defined (out of scope)
}

outerFunction();
"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Minimize the use of global variables to avoid naming conflicts and improve code maintainability."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use 'let' and 'const' for block-scoped variables to improve code clarity and prevent accidental variable modifications."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Understand the scope chain and how variables are resolved to avoid unexpected behavior."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Declare variables as close as possible to where they are used to improve code readability."}]
